; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; AUTHORED -- replicates a real rig (reference video IMG_2462: two YAM arms, a
; Dremel-style rotary tool clamped SIDEWAYS to a stand at the back of a mat --
; nose forward at the arms, fifteen degrees down -- a 25 mm polishing drum on
; a mandrel in its collet, and a small rest ledge on the column). The arms pick
; a white plastic strip off the mat, press its marked top face UP against the
; spinning drum's BARREL, and rub each blue mark away.
;
; WHAT MAKES THIS ONE INTERESTING. The bit is FIXED, so every mark has to be
; brought to it: the strip is carried up to the drum, pressed, RUBBED, lifted
; clear and SHIFTED along its own length before the next mark can reach it.
; Both hands hold it for all of that -- the marks sit at 0 and +-33 mm and the
; hands at +-58 mm, so a mark is reached by translating the whole rigid body,
; both arms with it.
;
; AND THE RUB HAS A DIRECTION. The drum works on its side, so its contact is a
; band 10 mm along the spindle and 12-15 mm across it -- against a mark that is
; 18 mm across the strip and 8 mm along it. Most of every mark is therefore out
; of reach of any one press: measured, a press held at any depth from 1 to 6 mm
; of overdrive clears 0.68 of a mark and then stops, against a gate of 0.95.
; The rest is reached by rubbing the strip ALONG the spindle axis, toward and
; away from the arms.
;
; Rubbing the other way -- sideways, along the strip's own long axis -- reaches
; nothing and cannot be held. Nothing, because the band is already wider than
; the mark in that direction. Cannot be held, because that is the direction the
; drum's surface runs in: read straight off the solver's contact table at a
; 3.3 N press, the tangential force is 3.45 N across the spindle and 0.00 N
; along it, so a rub one way costs the full mu * press Coulomb drag through an
; end-on pinch whose couple is only as wide as the strip is thick, and a rub
; the other way costs nothing the contact can supply. Neither fact is written
; into the removal law. Both are what this geometry does.
;
; The drag is also what the GRASP has to survive, and that is the hard part of
; this task. It is mu * press in the strip's own plane -- the same order as the
; pads' whole friction budget -- and the pads' budget is not a constant: a
; position-servoed finger presses with whatever standing position error the part
; holds its jaws open by, and every press erodes it. Measured over three marks
; with the grip left alone: 40 N at the close, 8 N after the first mark, and the
; strip on the bench during the third. Two things answer it, both recorded with
; their numbers in rgbs/tasks/rgbs/routine_polish.py -- a force HOLD on the fingers
; (``GripHold``) and a press at half the overdrive this first shipped with.
;
; Removal is a per-cell irreversible Preston-law field (rate = k_p * pressure
; * sliding speed) owned by the `marks` entity. Nothing here can un-polish. The
; law is isotropic and says nothing about direction: what a mark's cells see is
; decided entirely by whether the barrel's band has passed over them.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (problem POLISH_THE_MARKED_STRIP)


  (:language polish the blue marks off the plastic strip with the rotary tool
             then set it back down on its rest)

  ; The cell, the pairing (right works, left holds, arms solid), the control
  ; mode and the episode count are rgbs.suite's; this is the delta. So is the
  ; rig: rgbs_views@v1 is registered aimed at THIS variant's drum contact,
  ; because polish's overhead camera is the one the whole suite's rig was
  ; calibrated from, so `strip` stages it as is and every other family aims
  ; it at its own work.
  (:staging
    ; Contact-rich rigid press against a velocity-driven spindle; the probe
    ; feed needs MuJoCo's own contact table. See rgbs/tasks/rgbs/staging.py.
    (:physics polish_960hz@v1)
    ; Multi-phase: dual pick, carry, per-spot press/rub/poll rounds for three
    ; marks, set-down, release, retreat, then a 2 s settled hold. Both hands
    ; hold throughout. Generous on purpose -- the budget is sized for the rubs a
    ; harder-pressing or worse-aimed policy needs, not for the shipped one.
    (:max-steps 12000)
    ; The usbc bimanual's stiffened servos, for the same reason: pressing a
    ; rigid part against a fixture is task-space positioning under contact,
    ; and at the MJCF's own 300 gain the TCP lags ~10 mm -- more than the
    ; whole press stroke.
    (:arm-actuation (:ke 1600) (:kd 70))
    (:gripper-actuation (:ke 20000) (:kd 20))
  )

  (:objects
    strip - polish_strip
    ; The blue marks: state the STRIP CARRIES, published by the task's mark
    ; field entity, the way peel's film publishes its bond field. No rigid
    ; body answers to this name and no clause below reads it through one --
    ; published states only.
    marks - polish_markfield
  )

  ; Placed by their records: where each stands is polish/layout.py's
  ; arithmetic. The rails are two records because a record that builds a
  ; thing builds it in one place; their height is a measured decision, and
  ; the layout module says why.
  (:fixtures
    mat       - polish_mat
    rest      - parts_rest
    rail_near - polish_rail_near
    rail_far  - polish_rail_far
    machine   - rotary_polisher
  )

  ; No regions: the set-down is scored on contact with the rest the strip came
  ; off, not on a box in space. The rest is what holds the strip's ends clear of
  ; the mat so a hand can take it end-on at all -- see rgbs/tasks/rgbs/polish/layout.py.

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; The world an episode starts in, re-verified after settling at every reset.
  ;
  ; Only the marks are stated, deliberately. Two candidate clauses were
  ; measured false AT THE RESET INSTANT and true a dozen steps later, and an
  ; init that warns on every clean episode teaches people to ignore the
  ; warning:
  ; * `(spinning machine)` -- the environment zeroes every joint velocity
  ;   AFTER entity resets, so the drum re-spins from rest over the next
  ;   ~0.15 s (well before the arms reach anything, long after the check);
  ; * `(>= (contact-force strip mat) ...)` -- the force atom's 10-step
  ;   smoothing window is empty at verification and reads a dropout as zero.
  ; Both quantities stay observable every step below; the graph gates on
  ; `spinning` itself before it presses.
  (:init
    (marked marks)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; ONE LATCH OVER EVERYTHING: all marks polished AND the strip back on the
  ; mat, released by BOTH hands, settled -- held for two simulated seconds so
  ; a bounce or a lingering finger cannot buy the verdict.
  ;
  ; (>= (polished-fraction marks) 0.95)
  ;     The field is monotone by construction, so the latch is belt-and-braces
  ;     -- but `once` is still right: the verdict is about the episode, and
  ;     the strip's later travels must not be able to take it back.
  ;
  ; (>= (contact-force strip rest) 0.05) and (<= (speed strip) 0.02)
  ;     Set down ON THE MAT and at rest. The force atom smooths over ten
  ;     steps, so the solver's contact dropouts cannot restart the hold-for
  ;     clock.
  ;
  ; (not (gripped strip.body))
  ;     The work arm's jaws are not stalled at the strip's width.
  ;
  ; (> (hands-clear strip) 0.10)
  ;     BOTH hands away -- `tcp-distance` reads the work arm only, and this
  ;     episode ends with two hands to clear. 100 mm against an 85 mm jaw
  ;     span.  ; CAL
  (:goal
    (once
      (And
        (>= (polished-fraction marks) 0.95)
        (hold-for 2.0
          (And
            (>= (contact-force strip rest) 0.05)
            (<= (speed strip) 0.02)
            (not (gripped strip.body))
            (> (hands-clear strip) 0.10)
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Progress toward the polish gate, clipped, UNMASKED -- panel.task's form.
  ; A press-force budget belongs in a later `strip_gentle` variant, after the
  ; gt graph has measured clean-run and abusive-run populations; an
  ; uncalibrated threshold is worth less than the number it thresholds. The
  ; set-down clauses are deliberately NOT folded into the scalar: reward
  ; measures how much got polished; release is a condition on when the verdict
  ; counts.
  (:reward (progress (polished-fraction marks) (:gate 0.95)))

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Recorded every step, gating nothing -- the quantities this suite exists to
  ; calibrate. `press-force` is the future gentle-variant budget;
  ; `spots-remaining` is what makes a trace legible ("stalled at 2 remaining"
  ; vs "finished"); `disc-speed` shows the drum bogging down under a press.
  (:observe
    (spots-remaining marks)
    (press-force marks)
    (strip-on-bit marks)
    (disc-speed machine)
    (gripped strip.body)
    (hands-clear strip)
    (speed strip)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; A graph, and no routine: the per-mark shift choreography and the rub rounds
  ; polled against the field are closed-loop by construction, which is what a
  ; graph is for.
  (:artifacts
    (:tools "tools:register_polish_tools")
    (:graph gt "graphs/polish_gt")
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier original)
    (:source "Authored for this corpus from a reference video and photograph of
              the real rig: bimanual YAM arms, a Dremel-style rotary tool
              clamped SIDEWAYS to a vertical stand over a work mat -- nose
              forward at the arms and fifteen degrees down -- a 25 mm polishing
              drum on a mandrel, worked on its barrel, and a rest ledge the
              reference operator regrasps on. The ledge is modelled and
              reachable; this variant's schedule does not use it, having
              measured that it does not need to. The tool hung NOSE-DOWN in the
              first build of this task, presenting the drum's flat end face,
              which is not how a polishing drum is used and made the rub's
              direction unmeasurable.")
    (:note "Strip 150x25x4 mm rigid box; marks are three 8x20 mm patches spread
            along the top face -- WIDER ACROSS THE STRIP THAN THE DRUM IS LONG,
            which is what makes the rub necessary -- reached by shifting the
            whole strip under a fixed bit with both hands still on it. An
            earlier schedule put the strip down and regrasped between marks on
            the premise that the marks were spread wider than one grasp window;
            measured, they are not. A single hand does hold the strip,
            measured; what it cannot do is react the press torque at 78 mm of
            lever on a 4 mm-wide grip couple, nor the drum's drag through a rub
            -- see rgbs/tasks/rgbs/graphs/polish_gt/README.md.
            Removal is a per-cell irreversible field; polished-fraction is
            monotone, so `once` latches nothing the world could take back.
            CAL-tagged thresholds are re-measured against the shipped graph
            before sign-off.")
  )

)
